  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend is expected through Thursday with \nmostly dry conditions.\n\nThe Latest surface analysis depicted high pressure centered over the \nregion with mostly clear skies apart from some cirrus. Temps as of 2 \nAM were in the lower 60s across most of the area apart from upper \n60s along the coast and a few locations in the upper 50s. Given high \npressure overhead, mostly clear skies, and calm winds, expect \nradiational cooling to allow for temps to drop into the upper 50s \nfor most inland areas, lower 60s in urban areas, and mid-upper 60s \nalong the coast. A few rural locations may even drop into the mid \n50s. Another pleasant day is expected today with mostly sunny skies, \nhighs in the mid 80s for most (although a few locations may reach \nthe upper 80s), and dew points dropping into the upper 50s to around \n60F during the afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore this evening into tonight with winds \nbecoming southerly. However, the high will have more of an influence \nacross southern portions of the area where winds will likely become \ncalm overnight. As such, expect one more night of cooler \ntemperatures with lows in the upper 50s across southern portions of \nthe area as well as the Piedmont, lower 60s across the NE half of \nthe area, and mid 60s along the coast. SW winds increase Wed into \nThu in the warm sector of a strong surface low moving across Canada \nwell to the north. Meanwhile, the surface high moves off the \nSoutheast coast. Wind gusts up to around 20 mph are possible on Thu \nafternoon. Meanwhile, the ridge briefly moves into the region as \nwell which will help temps rise into the upper 80s for most Wed \n(locally mid 80s) and low-mid 90s by Thu. This will also allow for \nmoisture return as well with dew points increasing into the mid 60s \nto lower 70s by Thu. Meanwhile, dry conditions continue through at \nleast early Thu.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern develops late Thursday \ninto the weekend with daily chances for showers or storms.\n\nGiven increasing moisture and an approaching cold front, scattered \nshowers and storms are possible across mainly the northern portions \nof the area late Thu afternoon into Thu evening (30-60% PoPs north). \nA few strong to potentially severe storms are possible, but \nconfidence in severe weather is low given how far north the parent \nlow is and uncertainty regarding timing and the degree of moisture \n(and therefore instability) return. The cold front slowly moves \nsouth Thu night into Fri before stalling in NC. Given the proximity \nof the cold front Fri, additional scattered showers and storms are \npossible across southern portions of the FA (25-55% PoPs). The front \nslowly lifts back north Sat. Meanwhile, a shortwave moves into the \narea, potentially forming a weak surface low along the front. This \nwill allow for continued unsettled conditions into Sat with \nscattered showers and storms possible (15-45% PoPs NE and 50-65% \nSW). Given the proximity of the front, a few strong to potentially \nsevere storms are possible. However, confidence is low at this time. \nLow-end PoPs continue into Sun with the highest PoPs across eastern \nportions of the FA. Beyond this weekend, confidence in additional \nprecipitation chances remains low. Additionally, temps don't cool \nmuch behind the cold front Thu night with highs near to slightly \nabove normal from Fri into early next week.",
